@SimonBiggs Sorry - I didn't mean to discourage the bundling approach if it works for you and your audience! Pure Python code should work well of course, binary Python packages might need a bit more care, and then I'm thinking that non-Python dependencies such as Tensorflow would be hard work...

I was really talking of generic Streamlit apps which might make use of a broad range of technologies in their models. And I was also considering Mac, Win, and Linux which then increases complexity of code (whether bundling or not) compared to always targeting Linux through Docker.

Of course I realise that for your particular project you know it better than I ever could! I haven't played with the bundling approach for a few years to be honest so I'm happy to hear that things have worked well in your case.

@vdonato I was able reproduce the issue with the protobuf collisions between Streamlit and Supervisely. However after adding the `package streamlit;` line to all the protobuf definitions and rebuilding them, the front-end is now failing to compile because all of the protobuf imports are broken. The issue is with the JS protobuf generation using protobufjs - after adding the package name to the protobuf files, the auto-generated `proto.js` file now just exports `streamlit`, an object with all of the protobuf messages as properties, rather than the protobuf messages themselves.

Here's a quick snippet of the new auto-generated `proto.js` file if that's helpful:

```
// Exported root namespace
const $root = $protobuf.roots["default"] || ($protobuf.roots["default"] = {});

export const streamlit = $root.streamlit = (() => {          <--------- now wraps all protobuf types             

    /**
     * Namespace streamlit.
     * @exports streamlit
     * @namespace
     */
    const streamlit = {};

    streamlit.Alert = (function() {                        <----------- no longer directly exported

        /**
         * Properties of an Alert.
         * @memberof streamlit
         * @interface IAlert
         * @property {string|null} [body] Alert body
         * @property {streamlit.Alert.Format|null} [format] Alert format
         */

        /**
         * Constructs a new Alert.
         * @memberof streamlit
         * @classdesc Represents an Alert.
         * @implements IAlert
         * @constructor
         * @param {streamlit.IAlert=} [properties] Properties to set
         */
        function Alert(properties) {
            if (properties)
                for (let keys = Object.keys(properties), i = 0; i < keys.length; ++i)
                    if (properties[keys[i]] != null)
                        this[keys[i]] = properties[keys[i]];
        }
        .....
```
And the output after running the front-end:
```
Failed to compile.

./src/lib/utils.ts
Attempted import error: 'Alert' is not exported from 'src/autogen/proto' (imported as 'AlertProto').
```
I'm trying to understand more about Protocol Buffers and protobufjs, but I'm not finding much in the way of parameters we could pass to pbjs in the Makefile to prevent this. I didn't want to jump to changing all the imports across the entire front-end, so if you have any suggestions, I'd really appreciate it!

Hey @vdonato, I went through and created the `proto.ts` file like you described - as far as I can tell, there's no way to unpack and export everything in one go from the import, so I wrote up something like this:
```
import root, { streamlit as ProtoStreamlit } from "src/autogen/proto";

export default root;
export const Alert = ProtoStreamlit.Alert;
export const ArrowDataframe = ProtoStreamlit.ArrowDataframe;
export const ArrowTable = ProtoStreamlit.ArrowTable;
.
.
.
```
After that I went through and replaced all the `src/autogren/proto` imports with `src/proto` which fixed the issues for most of the files. However, there are still a few imports that are failing that I'm still figuring out, specifically the export from `src/autogen/proto.d.ts`. I'm sort of familiar with TypeScript but I'm not sure about how to unpack this declaration file so the imports work out across the front-end, for things like `IDeployParams` in `App.tsx` and elsewhere. I figured I'd check in to make sure this is the right track and if you have any advice for the `d.ts` exports. Thanks again!

> Is this mapped correctly onto the proto values? "stringValue" vs. string string_value = 6;

The conversion from `snake_case` to `lowerCamelCase` is done automagically for protobufs depending on the conventions of the language, so the mapping here is correct. 

I think a change may have recently gone in to fix this that'll be released in the next version. @AnOctopus can confirm whether this is the case.

I think this happens for any geojson data that is not defined only by the URL. I get the same error when trying to draw a map using a geojson dict passed directly to the function instead of one loaded from a URL.

Here is a minimal example, using very basic geojson data:

```
import altair as alt
import streamlit as st

#simple polygon data, raw from github
url_geojson = 'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mattijn/datasets/master/two_polygons.geo.json'
data_geojson_remote = alt.Data(url=url_geojson, format=alt.DataFormat(property='features',type='json'))

#chart object for remote data
ch_remote = alt.Chart(data_geojson_remote).mark_geoshape(
).encode(
    color="properties.name:N"
).project(
    type='identity', reflectY=True
)

#Same data, defined in python as a dict
var_geojson = {
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "features": [
    {
      "type": "Feature",
      "geometry": {
        "type": "Polygon",
        "coordinates": [[[0, 0], [1, 0], [1, 1], [0, 1], [0, 0]]]
      },
      "properties": {"name": "abc"}
    },
    {
      "type": "Feature",
      "geometry": {
        "type": "Polygon",
        "coordinates": [[[1, 0], [2, 0], [2, 1], [1, 1], [1, 0]]]
      },
      "properties": {"name": "def"}
    }
  ]
}


# inline geojson data object
data_geojson = alt.InlineData(values=var_geojson, format=alt.DataFormat(property='features',type='json')) 

# chart object using local data
ch_local = alt.Chart(data_geojson).mark_geoshape(
).encode(
    color="properties.name:N"
).project(
    type='identity', reflectY=True
)
st.write(ch_remote)
st.write(ch_local)
```

The result:
![4bd398c4c714035431ab35b9224b424e889a86e0](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/46822548/137482932-787b18a8-8d4d-426c-a908-6402a446b55d.png)

I haven't been able to dig into it with a debugger to watch what happens, but it seems that the geometry data gets lost when the dataset is cleaned from the chart object and streamlit attempts to convert it into a table. It happens with the legacy Altair (streamlit attempts to convert the data into a data frame) and the latest (streamlit attempts to convert the data into a ln arrow dataframe). As far as I can tell, the data is only defined in the `dataset` attribute of the chart, not the `data` attribute, so it may even be completely deleted when streamlit begins rendering the chart.

The question I have is why does it work when the json data is loaded fully from a URL? If that still works quickly, why not add some kind of condition to detect geojson data and skip the part where it tries to get converted into an arrow table? Surely passing simple data in memory isn't much worse than loading it from a URL?

